The method works by mapping the one-dimensional sequence of bases (A, C, T, G) onto a two-dimensional grid, where each base is assigned one of four colors. Adjacent cells of the same color are then merged into "macro-regions," creating a map that can be analyzed using graph theory and linear algebra (specifically, the eigenvalues of the dual graph's distance matrix). This provides a compact, visual, and numerical way to compare sequences and build phylogenetic trees without direct alignment.

